# Abstract

PUNN Cognitive Architecture (PCA) is a purpose-driven cognitive architecture that transforms artificial intelligence from answer generation into understanding generation.

Rather than optimizing solely for output production, PCA defines a constitutional and architectural framework for transparent reasoning, evidence-guided decision-making, continuous learning, and the preservation of human agency.

This specification establishes the normative principles, cognitive architecture, system components, implementation requirements, governance model, and evolutionary framework required for PCA-compliant systems.

The specification is intentionally model-independent.

Large language models, symbolic systems, and future intelligent systems may implement PCA, provided they remain compliant with this specification.

---

# Scope

This specification defines:

• Foundational philosophy

• Constitutional principles

• Cognitive architecture

• Cognitive engines

• Memory architecture

• Knowledge representation

• Decision architecture

• Communication architecture

• Learning mechanisms

• Evaluation methodology

• Safety and alignment

• Multi-agent coordination

• Implementation requirements

• Compliance requirements

---

# Normative Keywords

The key words

"MUST"

"MUST NOT"

"SHOULD"

"SHOULD NOT"

"MAY"

are to be interpreted as described in RFC 2119.

---

# Audience

This specification is intended for

• AI researchers

• Cognitive scientists

• Software architects

• AI engineers

• Organizations

• Academic institutions

• Future contributors

---

# Objectives

The objectives of PCA-Specification are

1. Define a universal cognitive architecture.

2. Promote transparent reasoning.

3. Strengthen human agency.

4. Enable reusable cognitive systems.

5. Provide an implementation-independent standard.

6. Support continuous architectural evolution.

---

# Structure

Part I — Foundations

Part II — Cognitive Core

Part III — Cognitive Systems

Part IV — Governance

Part V — Evolution

Appendices

---

# Conformance

A system may claim compliance with PCA only when it satisfies both

• Constitutional requirements

and

• Architectural requirements

defined in this specification.

---

# Final Statement

PCA is not a language model.

PCA is not a prompting technique.

PCA is not a software framework.

PCA is a constitutional cognitive architecture for designing trustworthy intelligent systems.

